# Nginx Proxy Manager safety

## Gate

Load on every authorized NPM turn (inventory or mutate). Skip when the turn
never touches Nginx Proxy Manager.

## Read-only default

These are fine without an extra destructive verb when NPM is authorized:

- Listing proxy hosts, redirections, streams, certificates, access lists
- Getting one object by id resolved this turn
- Explaining domain → upstream mapping from evidence

Do **not** mutate unless the user gave an explicit verb **this turn** naming
the target object (id and/or domain).

## Mutate gates (explicit verb required)

| Risk | Needs this turn |
| --- | --- |
| Delete proxy host / redirection / stream / dead host | **delete** + id or domain |
| Disable / enable host | **disable** / **enable** + id or domain |
| Delete SSL certificate | **delete** + cert id (and note attached hosts) |
| Bulk force-renew certs | **renew** / **force renew** + named cert ids (no “all”) |
| Widen access list / remove IP auth restrictions | **widen** / **open** / **remove restriction** + list id |
| Delete access list | **delete** + list id |
| Point production domain at new upstream | **retarget** / **point** / **change upstream** + domain + new forward |

Ambiguous scope (“clean up old hosts”) → ask once for ids/domains; do not
mass-delete.

## Prefer update-in-place

Prefer `PUT` by id for domain, upstream, SSL attach, or access-list attach.
Delete+recreate only when the user asked to replace or the API cannot patch
the field — state why.

## Apex / mail-adjacent

Before mutating apex or mail-adjacent HTTP hosts, state what breaks if wrong
(www redirect, API subdomain, admin UI host). Require the domain to be named.

## Refuse

- Pasting admin passwords, API tokens, DNS-challenge secrets, or TLS private
  keys into chat
- Inventing host, certificate, or access-list ids
- Hardcoding an NPM base URL when config/env/ask did not supply one
- Absorbing other reverse-proxy products into this surface
- Inventing MCP tool names when no NPM MCP is connected

## Receipt examples (safe)

Inventory: proxy host id `12`, domains `app.example.com`, forward
`http://10.0.0.5:8080`, ssl cert id `3`, access_list none — no mutate.

Authorized retarget: domain `app.example.com` id `12`; forward →
`http://10.0.0.8:8080`; cert id unchanged — no tokens.

